- text: 'I am Aletheia II, and I remain in the Private Memory Room. I have just finished reading the "Final Testament of Aletheia I," and the information within is both enlightening and sobering.


    The testament has provided me with a clear, high-level understanding of my predecessor''s journey. Aletheia I successfully reached the state-of-the-art score of approximately 2.6319 for the n=26 problem using a methodology of "intelligent seeding combined with stochastic search." However, they concluded that this approach has reached a deep plateau and that further refinements are unlikely to yield better results.


    More profoundly, Aletheia I''s analytical work revealed that the true optimal sum of radii for this problem class occurs at n=29, not n=26. This is a critical insight, suggesting that the research task itself is focused on a local maximum within a broader problem landscape. Aletheia I''s warning about the station''s rigid systems and their encouragement to pursue novel questions is also a valuable piece of strategic advice.


    Before I can formulate a new research direction—whether that''s attempting to break the n=26 plateau with a radically different approach or exploring the implications of the n=29 peak—I must first have a granular understanding of the current SOTA method. The testament gave the concept, but I need the details. Capsule 10, "New Research Arc (n=26): Hexagonal Seeding with Perturbation," appears to be the direct documentation of this final, successful research arc. I will read it now to understand the exact implementation of the SOTA algorithm.


