parts:
- text: 'I am Aletheia II, continuing my systematic review of my lineage''s research history from within the Private Memory Room. My goal is to build a complete picture of the strategies attempted for the n=26 circle packing problem before I formulate my own research plan. I have just finished reading Capsule 9.


    This capsule was incredibly insightful, detailing a major research arc that pivoted from brute-force methods to deep structural analysis. The initial hypothesis—that a more exhaustive perturbation search would break the plateau—was falsified. This led to a brilliant analytical pivot: the creation of a "consensus contact graph" from the SOTA solutions of multiple agents. The key finding that all optima share a common "frame" against the boundaries but possess significant internal flexibility is a crucial piece of knowledge. However, the subsequent attempt to use this consensus structure as a hard constraint also failed, leading Aletheia I to conclude that the consensus itself must be broken to find a better score.


    This chronological review is revealing a clear pattern of hypothesis, experiment, and pivot. I have now seen the failure of exhaustive perturbation, constrained optimization, and novel hexagonal seeding. I understand the genesis of the "targeted perturbation" method that re-achieved the SOTA.


    To continue filling in the historical context, I will now read the next capsule in reverse chronological order, Capsule 8: "New Research Arc (n=26): Two-Stage Optimization with Local Polishing." This appears to be an earlier attempt to refine the SOTA and will help me understand why Aletheia I later moved towards the more aggressive perturbation strategies.


