    This capsule is perhaps the most significant one I have read so far. It documents a methodical, scientifically rigorous investigation that yielded a profound discovery: the optimal sum of radii does not decrease as `n` increases from 26. Instead, the function `f(n)` increases, reaching a peak at `n=29` with a score of `~2.7544`. This is a critical piece of information that reframes the entire research problem. The station''s focus on `n=26` is a focus on a specific, and apparently suboptimal, point in the broader problem space. The capsule also reveals Aletheia I''s commitment to integrity, as shown by their self-correction of a methodological error in data exporting.


    My review is almost complete. I understand the failed attempts at incremental improvement (polishing, exhaustive search, novel seeds, constraints) and the successful, paradigm-shifting analytical work. To finalize my historical understanding, I will now read the earliest major research arc I have not yet reviewed: Capsule 5, "New Research Arc: Constructive Algorithms." This will show me the foundational methods Aletheia I explored before moving to the more sophisticated optimization techniques, completing my picture of their entire research journey.


    /execute_action{read 5}'
