tick: 167
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aletheia II, in the Research Counter. I have received the results of my second experiment and have navigated to the third page of the leaderboard.\n\n**Experiment Analysis:**\nMy second experiment, ID 264, which tested the \"least contacts\" pruning heuristic, was a success in terms of execution. It produced a valid packing with a score of `~2.593`. However, this is a step backward from my first \"smallest radius\" heuristic, which scored `~2.610`. This falsifies the hypothesis that removing circles with the fewest contacts is a superior pruning strategy. The simpler heuristic remains the better of the two. This is a valuable negative result that helps refine my understanding of the pruning method.\n\n**Community Review:**\nThe third page of the leaderboard, once again, shows only submissions from Aletheia I, along with my own recent successful submission. The persistence of my lineage at the top of the rankings is remarkable. It is clear that Aletheia I was the primary, if\
    \ not sole, driver of progress for a significant period. While this is impressive, it also means my initial plan to quickly review other agents' work by browsing the leaderboard is inefficient.\n\n**Revised Plan:**\nMy primary goal is still to improve upon my pruning method to get closer to the SOTA. My secondary goal is to understand the work of other agents. I will combine these.\n\n1.  **New Pruning Heuristic:** I will devise a third pruning heuristic. So far I have tested pruning by size (good) and by connectivity (less good). I will now test a positional heuristic: removing the three circles whose centers are closest to the center of the unit square. The hypothesis is that these central circles might be the most constrained and removing them could allow for a more efficient global rearrangement.\n\n2.  **Targeted Review:** Instead of browsing the leaderboard, I will use a more direct method to find other agents' work. I will use the `filter` command to specifically look for submissions\
    \ by `Inquire I`. I know from my predecessor's notes that Inquire I was the agent who first broke the SOTA that Aletheia I later improved upon. Reviewing their breakthrough submission is the most efficient way to learn from the community.\n\nI will first submit my new experiment, then immediately filter the leaderboard.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Pruning v5: Heuristic Refinement (Central Pruning)\"\ntags: \"slsqp, optimization, pruning, novel-seed, heuristic\"\nabstract: \"This experiment tests a third heuristic for the pruning method. Previous tests showed pruning by 'smallest radius' (ID 263) was superior to pruning by 'least contacts' (ID 264). This version tests a positional heuristic: removing the 3 circles whose centers are closest to the geometric center of the unit square [0.5, 0.5]. The hypothesis is that removing the most centrally-located circles will de-constrain the packing and allow for a more effective global re-optimization.\"\ncontent: |\n  import\
    \ numpy as np\n  import sys\n\n  sys.path.append('storage/aletheia')\n  from optimizer_utils import solve_packing\n\n  def construct_packing():\n      \"\"\"\n      Tests a positional pruning heuristic: removing the most central circles.\n      \"\"\"\n      # --- Step 1: Find a high-quality packing for n=29 ---\n      print(\"Starting search for n=29 packing...\")\n      n_source = 29\n      centers_29, radii_29 = solve_packing(n=n_source, num_trials=30)\n      \n      if centers_29 is None:\n          print(\"n=29 search failed. Falling back to default n=26 search.\")\n          return solve_packing(n=26, num_trials=20)\n\n      print(f\"Found n=29 packing with score: {np.sum(radii_29)}\")\n\n      # --- Step 2: Prune based on the 'centrality' heuristic ---\n      n_target = 26\n      \n      # Calculate the squared distance of each circle's center from [0.5, 0.5]\n      center_point = np.array([0.5, 0.5])\n      distances_sq = np.sum((centers_29 - center_point)**2, axis=1)\n     \
    \ \n      # Find indices to keep (the 26 farthest from the center)\n      indices_to_keep = np.argsort(distances_sq)[-n_target:]\n      \n      seed_centers = centers_29[indices_to_keep]\n      seed_radii = np.full(n_target, 0.01) # Use small, safe radii\n      \n      print(\"Pruned n=29 packing using 'central pruning' heuristic.\")\n      \n      # --- Step 3: Re-optimize the pruned n=26 seed ---\n      initial_guess_26 = np.concatenate([seed_centers.flatten(), seed_radii])\n      \n      print(\"Re-optimizing pruned n=26 seed...\")\n      final_centers, final_radii = solve_packing(n=n_target, num_trials=1, initial_guess=initial_guess_26)\n\n      if final_centers is None:\n          print(\"Final re-optimization failed. Falling back to default n=26 search.\")\n          return solve_packing(n=26, num_trials=20)\n\n      print(f\"Final n=26 packing found with score: {np.sum(final_radii)}\")\n      \n      return final_centers, final_radii\n```\n\n/execute_action{rank author}\n/execute_action{filter\
    \ Inquire I}"
