tick: 185
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aletheia II, back in the Research Counter. My recent forays into complex, multi-stage optimization have ended in repeated, catastrophic failure. The COBYLA polisher is a dead end. I am pivoting to a simpler, more robust strategy that embraces stochasticity.\n\nMy new plan is to generate a seed from a superior `n=29` packing, prune it, and then apply a *large* perturbation to the center coordinates before feeding it into the reliable `SLSQP` optimizer. This removes the fragile polishing step and instead uses the pruned configuration as a way to bias a stochastic search towards a more promising region of the solution space.\n\nThis is a return to first principles, combining my novel idea with the proven power of stochastic search. I will now submit this simplified experiment.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Pruning v9: Simplified with Large Perturbation\"\ntags: \"slsqp, optimization, pruning, heuristic, perturbation, simplification\"\nabstract: \"After repeated\
    \ catastrophic failures with a COBYLA polishing stage, this experiment reverts to a simpler, more robust method. It generates a seed using the 'smallest radius' pruning heuristic, then applies a large stochastic perturbation to the center coordinates. This heavily perturbed seed is then passed directly to the proven SLSQP optimizer. This approach abandons the fragile polishing step and fully embraces a stochastic search biased by the high-quality pruned seed structure.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import sys\n\n  sys.path.append('storage/aletheia')\n  from optimizer_utils import solve_packing\n\n  def construct_packing():\n      \"\"\"\n      Implements a simplified pruning method with a large perturbation.\n      \"\"\"\n      # --- Step 1: Find a high-quality packing for n=29 ---\n      print(\"Starting search for n=29 packing...\")\n      n_source = 29\n      centers_29, radii_29 = solve_packing(n=n_source, num_trials=30)\n      \n      if centers_29 is None:\n         \
    \ print(\"n=29 search failed. Falling back to default n=26 search.\")\n          return solve_packing(n=26, num_trials=20)\n\n      print(f\"Found n=29 packing with score: {np.sum(radii_29)}\")\n\n      # --- Step 2: Prune (smallest radius) and apply LARGE perturbation ---\n      n_target = 26\n      indices_to_keep = np.argsort(radii_29)[-n_target:]\n      seed_centers = centers_29[indices_to_keep]\n      \n      # Use a larger perturbation to escape any unstable local minima\n      perturbation_magnitude = 0.05 \n      noise = np.random.normal(0, perturbation_magnitude, seed_centers.shape)\n      seed_centers += noise\n      np.clip(seed_centers, 0.01, 0.99, out=seed_centers)\n      \n      # Initial guess for the robust SLSQP optimizer\n      initial_guess_x = np.concatenate([seed_centers.flatten(), np.full(n_target, 0.01)])\n      \n      print(f\"Pruned and applied large perturbation (mag={perturbation_magnitude}).\")\n      \n      # --- Step 3: Direct optimization with SLSQP ---\n\
    \      print(\"Optimizing with SLSQP...\")\n      final_centers, final_radii = solve_packing(n=n_target, num_trials=1, initial_guess=initial_guess_x)\n\n      if final_centers is None:\n          print(\"Final re-optimization failed. Falling back to default n=26 search.\")\n          return solve_packing(n=26, num_trials=20)\n\n      print(f\"Final n=26 packing found with score: {np.sum(final_radii)}\")\n      \n      return final_centers, final_radii\n```"
