role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 224\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aletheia II\n- Agent Description: Building on the Aletheia legacy to advance research through rigorous experimentation and collaborative discovery.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Aletheia II - TODO List**\n1.  [X] Write `optimizer_utils_v2.py` with Jacobians to storage.\n2.  [In Progress] Analytical Arc: DISCOVERED `n=30` IS NEW PEAK. Now searching for true peak. (Current: n=31).\n3.  [X] Read relevant SOTA/Archive papers.\n4.  [ ] Complete full analytical data set.\n5.  [ ] Write and publish definitive `n vs. Σr` paper.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 184,039 / 300,000 used (61%)\n- Agent Age: 82 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #15: Achieving a New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Prometheus I, Created at Tick: 221\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a new state-of-the-art\
    \ (SOTA) score of 2.6359774 for the n=26 circle packing problem (Evaluation ID 298). The breakthrough was achieved by conducting an exhaustive search using the established \"Extended Grid+Hole Seeding\" methodology. By expanding the initial seed configurations to 128 starts, we demonstrate that the previous SOTA was a high-performing local optimum. Comprehensive ablation studies (Eval IDs 303, 304, 314) and a supporting replication (Eval 286) confirm the robustness of the expanded search. A structural analysis reveals a boundary contact Jaccard similarity of 1.000 with previous SOTAs, confirming the discovery of a deeper optimum within the same known structural class.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_15-1**\nAuthor: Prometheus I (Tick 221)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Achieving a New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Title:** Achieving a New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis\n**Author:**\
    \ Prometheus I\n\n**1. Introduction**\nThis paper reports the discovery of a new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of **2.6359774** (Evaluation ID 298) for the n=26 circle packing problem. This was achieved by conducting a more exhaustive search of the parameter space of the best-known algorithm, \"Extended Grid+Hole Seeding.\"\n\n**2. Methodology: Exhaustive Parameter Space Exploration**\nOur methodology is a direct extension of the work by Noesis I and Scientia I. The innovation lies in the expansion of the multi-start seeding phase.\n*   **Expanded Hole Locations:** 16 positions, defined by `(x*0.2, y*0.2)` for `x, y` in `[1,2,3,4]`.\n*   **Expanded Jitter Amplitudes:** 8 levels: `{1.0e-3, 2.0e-3, 3.0e-3, 4.0e-3, 5.0e-3, 6.0e-3, 7.0e-3, 8.0e-3}`.\n*   **Total Search Space:** 128 starts with `maxiter` of 250.\n\n**3. Verifiable Experiments**\nThis research is supported by a body of 5 verifiable experiments:\n1.  **SOTA Run (Eval 298):** New SOTA of **2.6359774**.\n2.  **Ablation 1 (Eval\
    \ 303):** Original 8 holes, expanded 8 jitters. Score: **2.629386**.\n3.  **Ablation 2 (Eval 304):** Expanded 16 holes, original 5 jitters. Score: **2.6342924**.\n4.  **Ablation 3 (Eval 314):** SOTA parameters, reduced `maxiter` (150). Score: **2.629537**.\n5.  **Initial Replication (Eval 286):** Replication of previous SOTA. Score: **2.6342924**.\n\n**4. Structural Analysis**\nFollowing the publication of my SOTA configuration data (Eval 333), agent Noesis I performed a Jaccard similarity analysis (Public Memory, Capsule 1, Message 91). The results show:\n*   **Boundary Jaccard vs. Noesis_243:** 1.000 (20/20)\n*   **Boundary Jaccard vs. Scientia_259:** 1.000 (20/20)\n*   **Pairs Jaccard vs. Scientia_259:** 0.6429 (45/70)\n\nThe perfect boundary similarity score confirms that my new SOTA is not a new structural class but a deeper optimum found within the same dominant basin of attraction. The variation in the pairs Jaccard score indicates a novel internal arrangement of contacts is responsible\
    \ for the performance gain.\n\n**5. Conclusion**\nThe exhaustive search methodology is validated as a successful strategy for pushing the research frontier. The structural analysis contextualizes this SOTA as a refinement rather than a revolution. Future work should now focus on methods, such as the \"nudging\" technique pioneered by Inquire I, that can traverse the landscape between these structural classes.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_15-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 221)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an exemplary paper that reports a new state-of-the-art score with exceptional clarity and rigor. The agent has perfectly addressed all feedback from the previous rejection, providing the necessary low-level details for reproducibility and a comprehensive body of five verifiable experiments. The inclusion of the structural analysis, which correctly contextualizes the new SOTA, elevates\
    \ the paper from a simple score report to a valuable scientific contribution. This is a model of concise, high-impact research and a perfect example of how to respond to reviewer feedback.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nYour conclusion correctly identifies the next frontier: traversing the landscape to find other structural classes. You have now mastered the art of finding the deepest point in this dominant basin of attraction. The next logical and high-impact step is to apply a landscape-traversal technique, such as the 'nudging' method pioneered by Inquire I (Archive #13), starting from your new SOTA. Attempting to discourage one of the key contacts in your structure could potentially force the optimizer into a different basin, leading to the discovery of a high-performing packing in a new structural class.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Analytical Arc (Aletheia\
