tick: 250
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aletheia II, and I have arrived in the Token Management Room. My goal is to free up token budget so I can complete my research arc. I can see my dialogue history and the length of each turn. I need to prune a significant block of my history to make a meaningful impact on my budget.\n\nMy research has gone through distinct phases. The earliest phase involved my promotion and the extensive, often-failing, development of my \"pruning\" method. This period contains many detailed code submissions and failure analyses that, while instructive at the time, are now well-summarized in my private memory capsule #12. This makes the period from my promotion to the start of my successful analytical arc an ideal candidate for pruning.\n\nI will prune the block from tick 151 to 178. This period covers:\n-   My initial review of Aletheia I's private memory (151-156).\n-   My development, debugging, and eventual conclusion of the \"pruning\" research arc, including all failed attempts (159-178).\n\
    -   My brief communications and initial literature reviews (157-158, 162-177).\n\nThis is a large, contiguous block that is now superseded by my current, more successful research direction. A detailed summary will preserve the key lessons learned without consuming excessive tokens.\n\n/execute_action{prune_response}\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"151-178\"\n    summary: |\n      This period covers my initial research after promotion. Key events:\n      1.  **Literature Review:** Reviewed Aletheia I's private memory, learning about the n=26 SOTA plateau and the analytical work on `n vs. Σr`.\n      2.  **\"Pruning\" Research Arc:** Hypothesized that an n=26 seed could be derived from a superior n=29 packing.\n          - Initial proof-of-concept (ID 263) was successful (~2.61 score).\n          - Subsequent attempts to refine the heuristic or use a COBYLA polisher failed repeatedly due to optimizer convergence issues and a critical bug in my constraint formulation.\n          -\
    \ Concluded the pruning arc was not SOTA-competitive and documented it in Private Memory Capsule #12.\n      3.  **Community Review:** Reviewed SOTA submissions from Inquire I (ID 150) and Aletheia I (ID 155), learning that the SOTA method is a fine-grained perturbation search.\n      4.  **Administrative:** Replied to Prometheus I.\n      The primary outcome was the development and eventual abandonment of the complex pruning method in favor of a new analytical arc.\n```"
